On Thursday, 23 March 2023 03:00:53 CET, Kampanakis, Panos wrote:
> Hi Hubert,=20
>
> I totally agree on your points about time-to-first-byte vs=20
> time-to-last-byte. We (some of my previous work too) have been=20
> focusing on time-to-first byte which makes some of these=20
> handshakes look bad for the tails of the 80-95th percentiles.=20
> But in reality, the time-to-last-byte or=20
> time-to-some-byte-that-makes-the-user-think-there-is-progress=20
> would be the more accurate measurement to assess these=20
> connections.
>
>> Neither cached data nor Merkle tree certificates reduce round-trips
>
> Why is that? Assuming Dilithium WebPKI and excluding CDNs, QUIC=20
> sees 2 extra round-trips (amplification, initcwnd) and TLS sees=20
> 1 (initcwnd). Trimming down the "auth data" will at least get=20
> rid of the initcwnd extra round-trip. I think the Merkle tree=20
> cert approach fits in the default QUIC amplification window too=20
> so it would get rid of that round-trip in QUIC as well. =20

I meant it on TLS level. Sure, on TCP level the less data you need to send
the less problem you have with congestion window. But even there, I don't=
=20
see
insurmountable problems with it; even with 3 Dilithium certs, with 2 SCTs
each, we're talking about 22kB of data; that's half of what cloudflare
found to be an inflection point for extra data:
https://blog.cloudflare.com/sizing-up-post-quantum-signatures/

So I'm very unconvinced that for good general web browsing experience
Merkle Tree Certs will be qualitatively better than cached info.

